<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<policyDefinitionResources xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema"
                           xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
                           revision="1.0" schemaVersion="1.0"
                           xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/GroupPolicy/2006/07/PolicyDefinitions">
  <displayName>PresenceLock</displayName>
  <description>Self-learning presence detection that locks the workstation when the user has left.</description>
  <resources>
    <stringTable>
      <string id="SUPPORTED_Win10_Win11">Windows 10 (1903 or later) and Windows 11, .NET Framework 4.8</string>
      <string id="Cat_PresenceLock">PresenceLock</string>
      <string id="Cat_Learning">Learning period</string>
      <string id="Cat_Sensors">Optional sensors</string>
      <string id="Cat_Admin">Administration</string>

      <string id="ModePolicy">Operating mode</string>
      <string id="ModePolicy_Help">Automatic (default): the agent learns silently and switches itself to enforcing mode once the statistical safety gate is met (see "Learning period").

Force learning: never lock; keep learning. Use for pilots or to pause enforcement.

Force enforcing: lock immediately with the current profile, bypassing the gate. Recommended only together with a baseline profile.

Computer policy overrides user policy.</string>
      <string id="Mode_Auto">Automatic (learn, then enforce when safe)</string>
      <string id="Mode_ForceLearning">Force learning (never lock)</string>
      <string id="Mode_ForceEnforcing">Force enforcing (lock now)</string>

      <string id="Thresholds">Decision thresholds</string>
      <string id="Thresholds_Help">P(away) at which the 3-second soft dim starts, and at which the workstation is locked after the dim went unanswered. Defaults 75 % and 90 %. Higher values mean fewer dims for present users and slower locking after departure.</string>

      <string id="LearningPeriod">Learning period and safety gate</string>
      <string id="LearningPeriod_Help">The agent leaves learning mode only when ALL hold:
- at least the minimum number of days has passed (default 15);
- at least 30 confirmed departures were observed (manual Win+L or certain absence);
- over the last 200 idle episodes, the 95 % upper confidence bound of the rate at which it would have dimmed or locked a user who was actually present is at or below the target (default 10 %).

If the gate is still not met after the maximum number of days (default 30), the profile is NOT force-promoted. It stays in learning and is flagged "LearningExtended" for review (PresenceLock.exe --status).</string>

      <string id="ShowLockNotification">Show "would lock" notice during learning</string>
      <string id="ShowLockNotification_Help">Learning mode only. When the agent would have locked, show a small notification. Clicking it tells the agent the lock would have been wrong, which is a strong training signal and speeds up learning. Not configured: the user can toggle it from the tray menu.</string>

      <string id="AppLists">Additional application classes</string>
      <string id="AppLists_Help">Comma-separated process names (with or without .exe) added to the built-in lists. "Reading" apps are ones where users plausibly sit still (CAD, IDE, PDF viewers, video calls). "Leaving" apps are ones that often precede stepping away. Only process names are ever inspected, never window titles or content.</string>

      <string id="EnableAudioSonar">Allow ultrasonic presence sensing (learning only)</string>
      <string id="EnableAudioSonar_Help">Allows the optional 20 kHz echo/Doppler sensor during learning. This build ships the signal processing but no audio capture backend, so enabling it currently has no effect. When a backend is integrated: Windows shows the microphone-in-use indicator, the "Let desktop apps access your microphone" privacy setting must be on, and the sensor is never relied on in enforcing mode unless it was available in at least 80 % of learning episodes.</string>

      <string id="EnableBleRssi">Allow Bluetooth LE signal-strength sensing (learning only)</string>
      <string id="EnableBleRssi_Help">Allows the optional Bluetooth LE RSSI presence hint during learning. This build ships the logic but no BLE watcher adapter, so enabling it currently has no effect. The same 80 % availability rule applies before enforcing mode may use it.</string>

      <string id="AllowUserExit">Allow the user to exit the agent (disable = NIS2 mode)</string>
      <string id="AllowUserExit_Help">Enabled or not configured: the tray menu has an "Exit" item.

Disabled (NIS2 mode) — set this as a COMPUTER policy; the guard service only honours the computer policy:
1. No "Exit" item in the tray menu.
2. The agent's process denies termination to standard users: Task Manager "End task", taskkill /F and Stop-Process fail with "access denied". SYSTEM and elevated administrators keep full access.
3. A quit message posted by another program restarts the agent instead of ending it.
4. Resetting a profile (PresenceLock.exe --reset-profile) requires an elevated administrator.
5. A deleted profile, a profile demoted from enforcing to learning, or a restarted learning clock is restored from the guard's protected copy. Policy values are never read from the user-writable profile file.
6. With the PresenceLock Guard service installed (Install-PresenceLock.ps1 -Guard): if the agent disappears from an unlocked session anyway, that session is LOCKED within about 4 seconds and the agent is restarted. Each event is written to the Application event log, source "PresenceLock", event ID 1001 (1003 if locking failed).

Out of scope: local administrators, and input injected to fake activity (mouse jigglers, key-press "keep awake" tools).</string>

      <string id="AllowUserPause">Allow the user to pause the agent</string>
      <string id="AllowUserPause_Help">Enabled or not configured: the tray menu has "Pause" with the lengths and the 24-hour budget from "User pause limits". Disabled: no pause item.

A pause is handled like a running video: the learned model does not dim or lock, nothing is learned from the silence, and (unless "User pause limits" says otherwise) the absolute confirmation after "Hold limits" still applies, so a computer left during a pause is locked within HoldMax + grace. The NIS2 bound is unchanged.

Windows' own screen saver / inactivity lock settings are NOT affected by a pause.

Every pause start and end is written to the Application log (source PresenceLock, events 1015/1016, never rate-limited) with the user and the budget left.</string>
      <string id="UserPauseLimits">User pause limits</string>
      <string id="UserPauseLimits_Help">Pause lengths: up to three values in minutes, comma-separated (5-480), shown in the tray menu. Default 30,60,90.

Budget: the total pause time per rolling 24 hours, however it is split or restarted (default 120; 0 = no pause). A chosen pause is charged in full when it starts; ending it early switches protection back on but refunds nothing. The budget is stored in the user profile and, in NIS2 mode, merged with the guard's protected copy, so neither restarting the agent nor editing the file restores it. A script that keeps re-starting the pause (AutoHotkey or similar) can therefore only spend the budget, never extend it, and each attempt that succeeds is logged.

Keep the confirmation during a pause (default on): after "Hold limits" minutes without input the screen asks "are you still there?" and locks after the grace period if nobody answers. Turn off only for workstations where an unattended screen during the whole pause is acceptable; then only Windows' own lock settings apply during the pause.</string>
      <string id="AllowProfileExport">Allow profile export from the tray menu</string>
      <string id="AllowProfileExport_Help">Disabled: the export items are removed from the tray menu. Command-line export (PresenceLock.exe --export) is unaffected. Default: allowed.</string>

      <string id="ProfileExportDirectory">Collect profiles to a share</string>
      <string id="ProfileExportDirectory_Help">Once a day the agent writes the user's profile to this folder as COMPUTER_USER.json. Use a share where users can create files but not read others' files. Profiles contain only model weights and counters; no input data, window titles or content. Merge them into a fleet baseline with: PresenceLock.exe --merge-baseline out.json in1.json in2.json ...</string>

      <string id="DataRetention">Data retention (cleaning service)</string>
      <string id="DataRetention_Help">The agent and the guard run a cleaning service at start and every 24 hours. Nothing grows without bound and no administrator action is needed:
- agent.log: lines older than the log retention are removed (also limited to 2 x 256 KB);
- habit journal: episodes older than 42 days are removed even if nothing new is recorded (also at most 5000);
- crash leftovers (*.tmp) are removed;
- a last-known-good copy of the profile is refreshed; if profile.json is ever unreadable or oversized, the agent restores it from that copy and writes event 1013 instead of starting over;
- guard: protected copies of users who have not logged on for the protected-copy retention are removed (event 1006); a user returning after that starts a new learning period;
- guard: THIS computer's files in the collection share older than the collected-profile retention are removed, if the computer account has modify rights on the share; --merge-baseline ignores older inputs in any case.

Defaults: logs 30 days, protected copies 180 days, collected profiles 90 days.</string>
      <string id="BaselineProfilePath">Baseline profile for new users</string>
      <string id="BaselineProfilePath_Help">Path to a baseline profile (from --export-baseline or --merge-baseline). Users without a local profile start from these weights instead of the neutral defaults. Calibration is always reset: every user still has to pass the safety gate on their own data before enforcing.</string>
      <string id="Cat_Passive">Passive media/meeting hold</string>
      <string id="MediaHold">Pause on visible media playback</string>
      <string id="MediaHold_Help">Default: enabled. While some process holds a display-required power request (for Chrome/Edge: a video playing in a VISIBLE page; a minimised window or background tab releases it), the learned model is paused and the running idle episode is discarded without a training label. Without this, "idle 180 s" during a video is learned as "away", which both teaches the model wrong and makes the learning-period safety gate look better than it is.

Read via CallNtPowerInformation(SystemExecutionState), which needs no admin rights. Whether it aggregates other processes' requests is not documented: verify with PresenceLock.exe --probe-signals. Ignored on server operating systems (machine-wide there).

The hold is always bounded by "Hold limits".</string>
      <string id="MeetingHold">Pause while the microphone or camera is in use</string>
      <string id="MeetingHold_Help">Default: enabled. While a RUNNING process has the microphone or camera open (Teams, Zoom, Webex, a browser call), the model is paused as for media. Source: the CapabilityAccessManager ConsentStore in HKCU (undocumented; LastUsedTimeStop = 0). A claim only counts if the owning process is actually running. Joining a meeting and walking away is common, so this never blocks locking: it is bounded by "Hold limits".</string>
      <string id="HoldLimits">Hold limits (absolute backstop)</string>
      <string id="HoldLimits_Help">Maximum idle minutes (default 15) after which a visible confirmation dim is ALWAYS shown in enforcing mode, whatever the passive signals or the model say; if it is not answered within the grace period (default 15 s) the workstation is locked. The same confirmation is used when the certain-absence horizon (see "Learned horizon cap", 3-10 min by default) is reached without a lock. No workstation therefore stays unlocked longer than these two values combined after the last input.

Simulated trade-off (60 days, a user with 3 videos and 2 meetings a day): HoldMax 5 min: 74 % of media/meeting pauses get a dim, longest unattended time 238 s; 10 min: 49 % / 531 s; 15 min: 30 % / 831 s; 20 min: 13 % / 1096 s. Without passive signals: 53 % / 538 s (the learned horizon lets the model learn that silent watching is presence, so short breaks from that context can stay unlocked up to the learned-horizon cap; with the fixed 3-minute horizon of earlier versions: 90 % / 184 s).</string>
      <string id="LearnedHorizon">Learned horizon cap (silent work)</string>
      <string id="LearnedHorizon_Help">How long a SILENT user may sit without being asked "are you still there?", when the agent has learned that this user works silently in that context (for example studying a CAD model without touching anything).

The certain-absence horizon starts at 3 minutes. Where the user's own model only expects absence later (it has been taught by answered dims and quick unlocks that the user is present), the horizon moves out to that point, never beyond this cap and never beyond "Hold limits". A departure that looks exactly like silent work (the user just stands up) is therefore locked at the latest after this cap plus the confirmation grace period.

Default 10 minutes. Set 3 to keep the fixed 3-minute horizon of earlier versions.

Measured in simulation (Inventor user, studies of 1-8 min, 2 coffee breaks a day, half of them without any "leaving" cue): cap 3 min: 70 % of studies get a dim, unattended at most 195 s; 5 min: about 40 % / 315 s; 10 min: about 1 % / 615 s; 15 min: about 1 % / up to 915 s. Departures with cues (leaving app, abrupt stop) are locked within about 1-4 minutes regardless of the cap.

Quality is monitored: if false dims exceed the learning-period target in enforcing mode, event 1010 is written to the Application log (source PresenceLock); a cap that is too tight for a user shows up there.</string>
      <string id="HoldIgnoreApps">Applications that never cause a hold</string>
      <string id="HoldIgnoreApps_Help">Comma-separated exe names (with or without .exe) or package family name prefixes whose microphone/camera use is ignored. Use for tools that keep the microphone open permanently (noise suppression, virtual audio devices), which would otherwise hold every idle period until the backstop.</string>
      <string id="Cat_Habits">Habit learning</string>
      <string id="HabitLearning">Learn the user's routine</string>
      <string id="HabitLearning_Help">Default: enabled. The agent keeps a 42-day journal of the user's idle periods (start time and duration, nothing else) and learns two things: when this user usually leaves for long (for example lunch or going home), and at which hours the user is essentially never active.

Habits can ONLY bring the visible confirmation dim forward (see "Earliest habit confirmation"); they never lock directly and never postpone anything, so a wrong habit costs at most one mouse movement. They act only after their own verdicts have proven precise on this user (95 % lower bound of precision &gt;= 70 %, at least 20 verdicts); until then they run in shadow mode.

Measured in simulation (office routine, 7 weeks): departure late in the evening with a video left running locked after 135 s instead of 915 s; at ordinary going-home time the gain is small, because the first minutes of a departure look like a coffee break. Extra dims for present users: about 1.5 % of idle periods.

The journal is a presence timeline (personal data). It stays in the user's own profile folder (habits.json) and is never exported, collected or copied to the protected store. Disable to stop collecting it.</string>
      <string id="HabitEarlyConfirm">Earliest habit confirmation</string>
      <string id="HabitEarlyConfirm_Help">Minutes of idle (default 2) before a habit verdict may show the confirmation dim. The dim is followed by a lock only if not answered within the confirmation grace period.</string>
    </stringTable>

    <presentationTable>
      <presentation id="ModePolicy">
        <dropdownList refId="ModePolicy_Enum" defaultItem="0" noSort="true">Mode:</dropdownList>
      </presentation>
      <presentation id="Thresholds">
        <decimalTextBox refId="SoftDimThresholdPercent" defaultValue="75" spinStep="1">Soft dim at P(away) % </decimalTextBox>
        <decimalTextBox refId="LockThresholdPercent" defaultValue="90" spinStep="1">Lock at P(away) % </decimalTextBox>
      </presentation>
      <presentation id="LearningPeriod">
        <decimalTextBox refId="MinLearningDays" defaultValue="15" spinStep="1">Minimum learning days </decimalTextBox>
        <decimalTextBox refId="MaxLearningDays" defaultValue="30" spinStep="1">Flag for review after days </decimalTextBox>
        <decimalTextBox refId="TargetFalsePositivePercent" defaultValue="10" spinStep="1">Max false-dim rate % (95 % bound) </decimalTextBox>
      </presentation>
      <presentation id="AppLists">
        <textBox refId="ExtraReadingApps"><label>Reading apps:</label></textBox>
        <textBox refId="ExtraLeavingApps"><label>Leaving apps:</label></textBox>
      </presentation>
      <presentation id="UserPauseLimits">
        <textBox refId="PauseOptionsMinutes"><label>Pause lengths (minutes):</label><defaultValue>30,60,90</defaultValue></textBox>
        <decimalTextBox refId="PauseBudgetMinutesPerDay" defaultValue="120" spinStep="10">Budget per 24 hours, minutes </decimalTextBox>
        <checkBox refId="PauseKeepsConfirmation" defaultChecked="true">Keep the "are you still there?" confirmation during a pause</checkBox>
      </presentation>
      <presentation id="ProfileExportDirectory">
        <textBox refId="ProfileExportDirectory"><label>Folder (UNC):</label></textBox>
      </presentation>
      <presentation id="DataRetention">
        <decimalTextBox refId="LogRetentionDays" defaultValue="30" spinStep="1">Keep agent log lines, days </decimalTextBox>
        <decimalTextBox refId="ProtectedCopyRetentionDays" defaultValue="180" spinStep="10">Keep protected copies of absent users, days </decimalTextBox>
        <decimalTextBox refId="CollectedProfileRetentionDays" defaultValue="90" spinStep="10">Keep collected profiles, days </decimalTextBox>
      </presentation>
      <presentation id="BaselineProfilePath">
        <textBox refId="BaselineProfilePath"><label>Baseline file:</label></textBox>
      </presentation>
      <presentation id="HoldLimits">
        <decimalTextBox refId="HoldMaxMinutes" defaultValue="15" spinStep="1">Confirmation dim after idle minutes </decimalTextBox>
        <decimalTextBox refId="ConfirmGraceSeconds" defaultValue="15" spinStep="1">Lock if unanswered after seconds </decimalTextBox>
      </presentation>
      <presentation id="LearnedHorizon">
        <decimalTextBox refId="MaxLearnedHorizonMinutes" defaultValue="10" spinStep="1">Ask at the latest after silent minutes </decimalTextBox>
      </presentation>
      <presentation id="HoldIgnoreApps">
        <textBox refId="HoldIgnoreApps"><label>Ignore:</label></textBox>
      </presentation>
      <presentation id="HabitEarlyConfirm">
        <decimalTextBox refId="HabitEarlyConfirmMinutes" defaultValue="2" spinStep="1">Earliest confirmation after idle minutes </decimalTextBox>
      </presentation>
    </presentationTable>
  </resources>
</policyDefinitionResources>
